@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: reaching a service across the sandbox boundary — two directions, one built, and why the item covers two different mechanisms
' status: proposed 2026-09-10, not implemented; _spec/_plans/ is exempt from the SPEC-reference rule.
' would touch: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (cdpPublish, StartCDPViewport, permissions.network.allow)
' would touch: internal/sbx/run.go (RunConfig.Publish), internal/egress/plan.go (the add-host switch)
' would touch: internal/manifest/manifest.go (a capability, if a tunnel becomes one)
' would touch: packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh (ensure_project_tools — the CLIENT half)
'   the worked example of one direction _spec/_plans/claude-in-chrome-reachability.puml
'   what proveo built for it ........... _spec/defs/claudecode/chrome-bridge.puml
'   the host-side viewport ............. _spec/internal/sbx/browser-viewport.puml
'   the grammar an allow entry must use  _spec/internal/sbx/kit-domain-form.puml
'   what governs egress on sbx ......... _spec/internal/sbx/policy-baseline.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── AUTHORITATIVE SOURCES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
'   sbx ports : https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/sbx/ports/
'
' ── THE ITEM COVERS TWO DIRECTIONS, AND THEY SHARE NO MACHINERY ────────────
' "tools & localhost tunnels (e.g. psql, claude-in-chrome)" names one need and
' two mechanisms. Separating them is the first useful thing this plan does.
'
'   HOST -> SANDBOX   something on the operator's machine reaches a service
'                     INSIDE the run. `RunConfig.Publish` plus
'                     `sbx ports --publish`. BUILT, for exactly one port.
'   SANDBOX -> HOST   the agent reaches a service on the operator's machine.
'                     `host.docker.internal`, plus an allow entry. NOT BUILT.
'
' claude-in-chrome is the worked example of the first: `cdpPublish` derives one
' publish spec and `StartCDPViewport` holds the host end while the run lives. A
' dev server or a preview URL is the SAME SHAPE and simply has no caller.
'
' psql is the worked example of the second, and nothing in that direction exists
' beyond the raw plumbing.
'
' ── DIRECTION TWO IS NOT A PORT, IT IS A TRUST DECISION ────────────────────
' The plumbing is already there and already gated:
'   docker backend   `--add-host=host.docker.internal:127.0.0.1` with the
'                    host-reach add-on OFF, and `:host-gateway` with it ON. The
'                    switch exists and DEFAULTS TO CLOSED, which is the right
'                    default and should survive this work.
'   sbx backend      the target `host.docker.internal:<port>` has to appear in
'                    `permissions.network.allow`. kit-domain-form.puml settles
'                    the grammar: exact host and exact host:port are ENFORCED,
'                    so `host.docker.internal:5432` is expressible and matchable
'                    without widening anything.
'
' BUT THE ALLOWLIST IS THE WRONG MENTAL MODEL FOR IT. Every other entry in that
' list is an outbound destination on the internet, chosen because the agent needs
' a provider or a package registry. A host service is a DIFFERENT TRUST DOMAIN:
' it is the operator's own machine, usually unauthenticated because it only ever
' listened on loopback, and frequently holding production-shaped data. Granting
' `host.docker.internal:5432` is not "one more allowed host"; it is handing the
' agent a database that was safe because nothing could reach it.
'
' So it should be per-run and explicit — an opt-in the operator states, not a
' manifest default and not a side effect of a def declaring a tool.
'
' ── AND "TOOLS" IS A SEPARATE HALF FROM "TUNNELS" ─────────────────────────
' `psql` reaching a host database needs TWO unrelated things:
'   THE CLIENT   a `psql` binary in the image. That is an image-layer question
'                — or `ensure_project_tools`, which already installs on demand —
'                and it has nothing to do with reachability.
'   THE REACH    the tunnel above.
' Either without the other is useless, and they fail differently: a missing
' client fails immediately and legibly, while missing reach fails as a timeout
' or a 403 that reads like an egress bug. This tree has already spent a session
' misreading exactly that shape, so the two halves want separate diagnostics.
'
' ── THE SHAPE WORTH COPYING IS ALREADY IN THE MANIFEST ─────────────────────
' `hostBrowser: claude-in-chrome` is a CAPABILITY a def declares, off by
' default, that grants a specific cross-boundary reach. A tunnel is the same
' shape, and reusing it means the picker already has a row for it and the
' posture already has a place to print it.
'
' NOT DECIDED, and the first is the load-bearing one:
'   WHO NAMES THE PORT. A def cannot know the operator runs Postgres on 5432; an
'     operator should not have to know a def wants it. So the grant is probably
'     per-run (a flag or a prompt row) with the def only declaring that it CAN
'     use one — which is a different schema shape than hostBrowser.
'   WHETHER A SOCKET BEATS A PORT. A bind-mounted UNIX socket needs no inbound
'     network path at all, and Postgres, Docker and most host daemons offer one.
'     Smaller hole, same capability, and it sidesteps the allowlist entirely.
'   WHETHER PUBLISH SHOULD GENERALISE. `cdpPublish` is hardcoded to one purpose.
'     A dev-server preview is the same mechanism and would be the second caller,
'     which is the point at which a one-off becomes an interface.
title PLANNED — two directions, one built:\npublish out, and a trust decision in

package "the operator's machine" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "a host service\n""psql"" · a daemon on loopback" as SVC <<error>>
  component "the reach switch\nadd-host OFF by default" as SW <<app>>
  component "the host end of a publish\n""StartCDPViewport""" as VIEW <<app>>
}
package "the sandbox" as SB <<host>> {
  component "the agent" as AG <<app>>
  component "a service in the run\nCDP today; a dev server next" as INSVC <<app>>
  component "the CLIENT binary\nimage layer or ""ensure_project_tools""" as CLI <<app>>
}
component """permissions.network.allow""\n""host.docker.internal:5432""" as ALLOW <<app>>

INSVC ARROW_MAIN VIEW : ""Publish"" + ""sbx ports"" — BUILT,\nfor exactly one port
AG ARROW_OPTIONAL SW : NOT BUILT
SW ARROW_MAIN SVC
ALLOW ARROW_MAIN SW : exact host:port — enforced\ngrammar, no widening
CLI ARROW_MAIN AG : useless without the reach
AG ARROW_ERROR SVC : an unauthenticated daemon that\nwas safe because nothing\ncould reach it

note bottom of SVC
  **A different trust domain.** Every other allow
  entry is an internet destination the agent
  needs. A host daemon listened on loopback
  because it was unreachable, and is usually
  unauthenticated for exactly that reason.
end note

note bottom of CLI
  **Two halves that fail differently.** A missing
  client fails immediately and legibly; missing
  reach fails as a timeout or a 403 that reads
  like an egress bug. This tree has already lost
  a session to that misreading.
end note

note bottom of ALLOW
  **The grammar is not the problem.** Exact
  ""host:port"" is an ENFORCED sbx pattern, so
  ""host.docker.internal:5432"" matches without
  widening anything. The question is whether it
  should be granted, not whether it can be said.
end note

note right of VIEW
  **One caller is a one-off.** ""cdpPublish"" is
  hardcoded to CDP. A dev-server preview is the
  same mechanism, and the second caller is the
  moment this becomes an interface rather than a
  special case.
end note

legend bottom
  **The order to do it in**
  ==
  **1. Split the item.** HOST -> SANDBOX is a publish and is built for one port;
  SANDBOX -> HOST is a trust grant and is not built. They share a sentence and no
  machinery, and treating them as one feature is how the wrong default gets shipped.
  --
  **2. Keep the closed default.** The docker add-host switch already defaults to closed.
  Whatever this becomes, granting the agent a host service stays an explicit per-run
  opt-in — not a manifest default, and not a side effect of a def declaring a tool.
  ==
  **3. Try a socket before a port.** A bind-mounted UNIX socket needs no inbound network
  path, and Postgres and Docker both offer one. Smaller hole, same capability, and it
  never touches the allowlist.
  --
  **Left open**: who names the port, since a def cannot know the operator's local setup
  and the operator should not have to know a def's needs; and whether ""Publish"" should
  generalise, which the second caller will decide.
end legend
@enduml
